 #:seon.task{:name "an-error-recorded-while-showing-a-form-rides-the-next-form",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "MCP SCI eval_clj of \"(+ 1 2)\\n#:seon.repl{:value 3}\\n(* 2 3)\": the reserved map was refused through :seon.batch/refused, a carrier with no :seon.batch/error-value, so seon.turn/show-row passed nil to seon.sci.eval/shown-failure; that failure was recorded while form 2 was shown and delivered as an error on form 3, whose value 6 was correct. Correct: a refused form is its own read error carrying its error value, and an error recorded while showing one form is delivered on that form. Proof: seon.cluster.evaluate-source-test/code-evaluates-as-one-ordinary-run-of-its-agent (the read-error testing block: no receipt carries a diagnostic).",
            :must-read #{seon.batch/evaluate-batch seon.turn/show-row}}]
